  "Attributes": [
    {
      "Id": 1266162,
      "attributeType": "LongString",
      "attributeName": "AIDescription",
      "attributeRelationType": "None",
      "Value": "\"168-in-1\" was a hugely popular cartridge for the MT-777DX console (a clone of the Nintendo Entertainment System) prevalent in Poland during the early 1990s. It wasn't a single game, but a compilation of 168 different titles, offering an incredible variety of gameplay experiences at a budget-friendly price. The cartridge itself was known for its robust, high-quality construction, initially featuring a distinctive purple casing. \n\nThe selection of games was diverse, ranging from action and adventure to puzzle and sports titles. While the exact composition varied between versions, it often included recognizable titles like *Contra* and *Super Mario Bros*, prominently advertised on the cartridge label. A rear label provided a microscopic list of all included games, a significant benefit for potential buyers. \n\nUpon startup, players were greeted with a large *Contra* logo accompanied by an unexpected, yet surprisingly fitting, soundtrack from the game *Booby Kids*.  \"168-in-1\" became a cultural phenomenon, effectively acting as a promotional tool for the Pegasus console, much like *Tekken* boosted PlayStation sales."
    },
